<!--
    GH-845: a 4-byte wide string literal (wchar_t on Linux, char32_t) passed
    to the image's own function printed as its address.

    Read 1 or 2 bytes at a time, L"hellow" (68 00 00 00 65 00 00 00 ..) is a
    one-character string, so no string fact is planted there: operand_refs
    declines its one-character read (#870), and the call prints the address,
    hashw(0x402004). The widestrings32 option (stage P1, code/data
    partition) adds the 4-byte width of the string markup and plants a
    wchar4[7] at the literal, which the printer renders as L"hellow".

    Pass 1 (option widestrings32 off): the address (#1).
    Pass 2 (option widestrings32 on): the literal (#2) and the L"(NULL)"
    default of ornull (#3), while the int tables keep their names (#4).
    Pass 3 (on, the clang build): L"hellow" follows the last unit of code's
    switch table, 'q', which nothing names, so the operand pointing at
    L"hellow" starts the literal (#5), and the table itself, which code
    indexes, keeps its address although its bytes spell L"helpz" (#6).

    operand_refs is on in every pass, as in the aggressive preset that small
    images default to.

    Source: decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/widestr32.c
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
